= Uncertainty =

'''Uncertainty describes limits on what is known, measured, predicted, defined, or inferred.''' It is not merely a lack of confidence. A claim can be strongly supported yet imprecise about magnitude or applicability, while a precisely stated hypothesis can remain poorly supported.

An honest account names the source of uncertainty, its practical importance and, when possible, the conditions under which it could be reduced. Uncertainty should not be added as a vague disclaimer when the evidence supports a definite conclusion.

== Sources and forms of uncertainty ==

The following distinctions are useful but not an exhaustive or mutually exclusive taxonomy.

=== Epistemic uncertainty — incomplete knowledge ===

Uncertainty arising from missing evidence, incomplete understanding, untested assumptions, contested explanations or an unknown relationship between observations.

Examples include a poorly constrained causal mechanism, inadequate historical records, or insufficient observations to distinguish two models. More appropriate evidence or analysis may reduce some of this uncertainty.

=== Methodological and measurement uncertainty ===

Uncertainty introduced or amplified by study design, sampling, instruments, operational definitions, missing data, computation, model specification and inferential choices.

It may be quantifiable as measurement error, confidence or credible intervals, estimated bias or sensitivity to assumptions. These quantities have different meanings; a confidence interval is not automatically the probability that a specific fixed parameter lies within the realized interval.

A method can be precise without being valid for the intended construct, and highly repeatable measurements can still be systematically biased.

=== Ontological and conceptual uncertainty ===

Uncertainty about whether an entity exists, what kind of thing it is, or where a conceptual boundary belongs. For instance, two scientific accounts may disagree on whether a proposed theoretical object corresponds to a distinct entity.

This meaning of '''ontological uncertainty''' should not be confused with '''aleatory variability''', which is often used for stochastic variation in a system. Whether uncertainty is reducible depends on its source and the available modeling assumptions.

=== Semantic and interpretive uncertainty ===

Ambiguous words, contested definitions, translation, variable disciplinary meanings and divergent interpretations can make a proposition appear more or less settled than it is.

Clarifying whether “information” means a Shannon quantity, a biological signal or ordinary communication can resolve a disagreement in terminology without producing new experimental data.

=== Variability and irreducible randomness ===

Some systems display variation not eliminated by improved knowledge of the current model or available measurement. Statistical models may represent this as aleatory uncertainty, in contrast to epistemic uncertainty about parameters or mechanisms.

The distinction is model-dependent: what is treated as irreducible in one analysis can become explainable after new observations, instruments or theories.

== Scope and time ==

Evidence may justify a finding only for a specified population, location, period, software version, instrument, experimental condition or parameter range. Extrapolation outside those conditions creates additional uncertainty, even if the result inside them is robust.

Some information is necessarily time-sensitive. “The system supports X” should specify a version or verification date when implementation can change. A historical statement should use the relevant period rather than an unqualified “currently.”

== Communicating uncertainty ==

{| class="wikitable"
! Insufficient expression !! More informative expression
|-
| “Studies disagree.” || “Results differ by population and measurement method; the available studies do not isolate which factor explains the discrepancy.”
|-
| “This might work.” || “The intervention produced the reported effect under the tested conditions; effectiveness outside those conditions has not been established.”
|-
| “The effect is significant.” || “The estimated effect is X with interval Y under model Z”; distinguish statistical significance from practical importance
|-
| “The software is reliable.” || “The tested release passed checks A and B under conditions C; production reliability has not been measured.”
|}

Specificity matters more than hedging. State the uncertainty that actually affects interpretation rather than adding “possibly” to every sentence.

== Quantitative and qualitative expression ==

Where methods justify quantification, appropriate representations include measurement uncertainty, confidence intervals, posterior or forecast probabilities, sensitivity ranges, bounds and scenarios. Their interpretation depends on the statistical framework and assumptions used.

When quantification is unsupported, explain the available evidence, principal alternatives and missing information in ordinary language. Numbers invented for a status dashboard are not a substitute for calibrated uncertainty.

Some disciplines have explicit conventions. For example, the [https://www.ipcc.ch/report/ar6/wg1/chapter/chapter-1/ IPCC uncertainty framework] distinguishes '''confidence''' in an assessed finding, informed by evidence and agreement, from '''likelihood''' expressed probabilistically. It illustrates transparent domain-specific calibration; its terminology should not be silently imposed on every BITwiki article.

== Uncertainty and evidentiary standing ==

The [[BITwiki:Epistemic status|epistemic status]] of a claim concerns its current evidentiary support; uncertainty describes the limits, ambiguities and variability that remain.

A well-supported experimental result can have uncertain effect size, untested boundary conditions or an incomplete mechanistic explanation. A hypothetical claim can be clearly defined even though no convincing evidence yet supports it. These distinctions can coexist in one article without contradiction.

== Uncertainty and decisions ==

Decision-making may be necessary before all uncertainty disappears. The relevant questions include:

# Which decisions are sensitive to the unknowns?
# What is the consequence of an incorrect conclusion?
# Which uncertainties could be reduced with additional evidence or testing?
# What observations would discriminate between the leading alternatives?
# Which actions remain sensible across plausible scenarios?

High-stakes domains—such as medicine, infrastructure, finance and safety—may justify more formal decision analysis and caution than ordinary descriptive material. Risk, which combines possible outcomes and consequences, is not identical to uncertainty about those outcomes.

== Updating and correcting an account ==

An uncertainty statement should be revised when measurements improve, new studies appear, a theory is challenged, a replication fails, a source is corrected, or the conditions of applicability change.

Do not use uncertainty to imply that all interpretations are equally plausible, or certainty to conceal material limits. Preserve the distinction between an established result and open questions about its explanation or scope.

